  "summary": "Recent preprints and a published study add exact simulation, growth-rate inference, and noise decomposition for stochastic biological models.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is stochastic modeling that treats biological variability as a quantitative object rather than residual error. A review of stochastic gene expression models for growing and dividing cells addresses cell-cycle fluctuation signatures and cyclo-stationary behavior, while a preprint on bacterial fitness develops branching-process and particle-filtering algorithms to estimate population growth rates from agent-based single-cell models. GeneRegulatorySystems.jl applies a biophysical chemical master equation for exact stochastic simulation of nonlinear gene regulation at cellular scale. The published yellow fever study applies global sensitivity analysis to separate noise channels in a stochastic epidemic model. The newer contribution across these items is the pairing of exact or inference-oriented computational methods with explicit decomposition of stochastic sources, spanning intracellular, cellular, and population scales. The items differ in scale and system, so the shared element is methodological rather than a common biological context.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-06"
}
